libjtty — the JTTY coding, by Oleg Belousov, R1CBU.

    https://github.com/strijar/libjtty        GPL version 3, see COPYING

These are the files of the library's src/ and include/ folders at its
commit 74025b2 of 28 September 2026, "Memory optimization, stream
decoder", put side by side in one folder. They are not changed here.
NOTICE is the library's own account of where it comes from: it was written
in C from WSJT-X 3.2.0-rc1, whose JTTY is in Fortran, and links nothing of
WSJT-X. PROTOCOL.md is its account of the protocol.

JTTY is WSJT-X's mode for typed text, from version 3.2: four tones 31.25 Hz
apart, a frame of 1.888 seconds to every five characters or so, and no
slots, so that an over begins when the operator sends it. What WSJT-X
3.3.0-beta4 sends is what 3.2.0-rc1 sent: between the two the files of the
protocol, lib/jtty/jtty_spec.f90, the coding and the waveform, are the
same, and the source coding gained only a way to send a message in pieces
and a code for text it cannot send.

    registry    the alphabet, the control phrases, the ARRL sections
    callsign    callsigns packed as FT8 packs them, in 28 bits
    atom        each frame's 34 bits: a callsign, an exchange, a phrase, or
                five characters of text
    text        packs a text into the fewest frames, and back
    crc         the 12 bit check on a frame
    fec         the error correction: a tail-biting convolutional code,
                and its Viterbi decoder
    modulator   the tones made into sound, at 12000 a second
    synchronizer, demodulator, receiver
                find the frames in a stream of sound, and read them

What the radio has of it is in modems/modem_jtty.c, which says how it is
used. The sound sent is not made with the library's modulator, which works
at 12000 a second, but at the sound card's rate with ft8_signal.c's
synth_gfsk(), which makes the same signal: JTTY's tones glide as FT8's do.

Measured against WSJT-X by its author, the library reads half its frames
at -14.6 dB, where WSJT-X reads half at -16.0 dB; it does not take out a
signal it has read to look under it, as WSJT-X does.
